  "hints": [
    "The last arrival time is the largest shortest-path distance from source. Weights are nonnegative.",
    "Dijkstra's algorithm settles nodes in increasing distance using a min-heap of (distance, node).",
    "Skip heap entries that are stale (distance larger than the best recorded). Afterwards, any unreached node means -1; otherwise return the maximum distance."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function lastArrival(n, edges, source) {\n  const graph = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [])\n  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) graph[u].push([v, w])\n  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity)\n  dist[source] = 0\n  const heap = [[0, source]]\n  const push = item => {\n    heap.push(item)\n    let i = heap.length - 1\n    while (i > 0) {\n      const p = (i - 1) >> 1\n      if (heap[p][0] <= heap[i][0]) break\n      ;[heap[p], heap[i]] = [heap[i], heap[p]]\n      i = p\n    }\n  }\n  const pop = () => {\n    const top = heap[0]\n    const last = heap.pop()\n    if (heap.length) {\n      heap[0] = last\n      let i = 0\n      for (;;) {\n        const l = 2 * i + 1\n        const r = l + 1\n        let m = i\n        if (l < heap.length && heap[l][0] < heap[m][0]) m = l\n        if (r < heap.length && heap[r][0] < heap[m][0]) m = r\n        if (m === i) break\n        ;[heap[m], heap[i]] = [heap[i], heap[m]]\n        i = m\n      }\n    }\n    return top\n  }\n  while (heap.length) {\n    const [d, u] = pop()\n    if (d > dist[u]) continue\n    for (const [v, w] of graph[u]) {\n      if (d + w < dist[v]) {\n        dist[v] = d + w\n        push([dist[v], v])\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  const latest = Math.max(...dist)\n  return latest === Infinity ? -1 : latest\n}\n",
    "py": "import heapq\n\ndef last_arrival(n, edges, source):\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for u, v, w in edges:\n        graph[u].append((v, w))\n    dist = [float(\"inf\")] * n\n    dist[source] = 0\n    heap = [(0, source)]\n    while heap:\n        d, u = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if d > dist[u]:\n            continue\n        for v, w in graph[u]:\n            if d + w < dist[v]:\n                dist[v] = d + w\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (dist[v], v))\n    latest = max(dist)\n    return -1 if latest == float(\"inf\") else latest\n",
    "explanation": "With nonnegative weights, the node popped with the smallest tentative distance can never be improved later, which is why Dijkstra is correct. Lazy deletion (skipping stale heap entries) avoids needing a decrease-key operation. The answer is the farthest settled node."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O((n + m) log m)",
    "space": "O(n + m)",
    "notes": "Negative weights break Dijkstra; Bellman–Ford handles them in O(n · m). Math.max(...dist) is fine for 10,000 values but use a loop for very large arrays."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Some links are unreliable with a known drop probability. How would you find the most reliable route?",
    "How does a real system (BGP, OSPF) compute routes when the graph keeps changing?"
  ]
}
